 E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | The docs-authoring epic is blocked by every Implementation epic; documentation describes what was built. |\n| (no edge) E002 ∥ E003 | Rule 14/15 | Different repos and surfaces. The stable API shields the Dashboard views from metering-schema drift, so the Dashboard work does not need the schema integration to finish — parallel. |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Status | Notes |\n|-----|----------|---------|--------|-------|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | E001, E002, E003 | Covered | Split by boundary: computation + stable API (E001, Distributed Workloads), administrator view (E002, Dashboard). Real-data delivery completes with the Tier-2 integration (E003). |\n| HLR-2 | P1 | E001 | Covered — **bundled** | CSV export and the stable API are retrieval formats of the same per-project GPU-hour report data and API surface the P0 computation produces. Splitting would make two epics build the same data structure and API endpoint; the P1 label reads as relative value, not a deferral signal. |\n| HLR-3 | P1 | E002 | Covered — **bundled** | The current-month-vs-Kueue-quota view renders the same consumption data in the same administrator view surface as the P0 report view and reuses its project scoping — it is not independently schedulable, so it stays bundled with the P0 view work. |\n| HLR-4 | P2 | E004 | Covered | docs-authoring epic; priority derived from strategy `priority: Major` → P1 per the docs-authoring priority exception (not an inversion — see Health Warnings). |\n\nAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nThe single external dependency — the metering data and its schema, owned by the in-flight RHAISTRAT-9990 strategy — has uncertain timing. Partial value is deliverable without it, so the strategy splits:\n\n- **Tier 1 (independent, starts now)**: E001 (report backend, stable API, CSV export) and E002 (Dashboard admin views), built and validated against the metering data contract as currently drafted by RHAISTRAT-9990. Risk-reduction value: the reporting stack exists and is tested before the metering data lands.\n- **Tier 2 (dependency-gated)**: E003 — reconciliation with the finalized metering data schema and end-to-end validation on real metering output. Gated on RHAISTRAT-9990 landing; modeled as a precondition with tiered delivery (Rule 21: third party resolves — no separate epic for the dependency itself, no Investigation epic).\n\n## Open Question Handling\n\nThe strategy's single open question — \"the metering data schema finalizes only when RHAISTRAT-9990 lands\" — was evaluated against the Step 3 decision rule. The answer does not change **which** epics exist (backend, views, integration, docs all exist regardless), and the resolution is produced by a third-party strategy, not by any investigation this team can run: no readable artifact holds the final answer yet, and the gating event is external coordination. Per Rule 21 this is modeled as a precondition with tiered delivery (see above) rather than an Investigation epic. The residual risk — schema drift between draft contract and final schema — is absorbed by E003, whose scope exists precisely to reconcile that drift.\n\n## Component Mapping Notes\n\n- The strategy lists affected components (Dashboard, Distributed Workloads) without per-component change or owner detail. Owners were taken from the architecture context files (`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/dashboard.md`: RHOAI Dashboard; `.../distributed-workloads.md`: Distributed Workloads).\n- **Report backend assigned to Distributed Workloads (non-obvious mapping):** the reporting backend (per-project GPU-hour aggregation, stable API, CSV export) is assigned to the Distributed Workloads canonical component because it computes over workload GPU consumption and the Kueue quota model — the Distributed Workloads domain. `dashboard.md` states the Dashboard \"owns no data plane\" and uses \"Kubernetes API only; no private component APIs\", so a time-range aggregation API is not a Dashboard-internal artifact. This epic's work spans the strategy components Dashboard + Distributed Workloads; Distributed Workloads is the single most-relevant canonical name.\n- **HLR-3 quota side:** the Dashboard reads Kueue quota through its existing CR-based integration model (per `dashboard.md`), so no separate Distributed Workloads epic is needed to expose quota — quota-semantics correctness is an acceptance criterion on the Dashboard epic.\n- **Documentation component:** Documentation is a canonical Jira component with an established Red Hat docs content process; this is content authoring, not provisioning of a new code component, so no onboarding chain is generated (Rule 20 targets new platform components).\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n1. **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no Affected Components change/owner detail, no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, and no Non-Functional Requirements. Epic-level ACs were derived from the HLRs; team assignments came from architecture context. Human verification of team routing recommended.\n2. **External timing risk**: E003 completes only when RHAISTRAT-9990 lands and its schema is final. That schedule is outside this strategy's control; Tier 1 (E001, E002) deliberately proceeds in parallel against the draft contract so the strategy is not blocked.\n3. **\"Stable API\" commitment (HLR-2)**: the strategy requires a stable API for finance tooling but defines no versioning process. The API design in E001 should treat stability/versioning as an explicit design concern; flagged as a scope consideration, not a defect.\n4. **Docs priority reads as an inversion but is not one**: HLR-4 is P2 while the docs epic is P1 — this follows the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy priority Major → P1) and does not push priority onto the implementations.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":35766,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-af6bc5e1638796b3","agent_id":"a1cfebc54f0a2f4fa","session_id":"9bcc61a9-70a5-417c-9026-158475c20fef","uuid":"ecfeb7a8-c7c1-4648-b7f5-635db426116f","timestamp":"2026-10-23T19:50:19.810Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9018 into epics"}
