{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-99d16a4811493313","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9018\n3\tepic_count: 4\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9018 (GPU chargeback reporting for platform administrators)\n11\t\n12\t## Epic List\n13\t\n14\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n15\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n16\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E001 | GPU-hour chargeback report backend with stable API and CSV export | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P0 |\n17\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E002 | Administrator GPU chargeback reporting views in the Dashboard | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P0 |\n18\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E003 | Metering schema integration and end-to-end chargeback report validation | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P0 |\n19\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E004 | Chargeback reporting documentation: report semantics and export integration | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Documentation | P1 |\n20\t\n21\t## Dependency DAG\n22\t\n23\t```mermaid\n24\tgraph TD\n25\t    E001[\"E001 Report backend + API + CSV\"] --> E002[\"E002 Dashboard admin views\"]\n26\t    E001 --> E003[\"E003 Metering schema integration\"]\n27\t    E001 --> E004[\"E004 Documentation\"]\n28\t    E002 --> E004\n29\t    E003 --> E004\n30\t```\n31\t\n32\t## DAG Justification\n33\t\n34\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n35\t|------|------|-----------|\n36\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 13 (build-against artifact) | The Dashboard view code builds against the stable reporting API produced by the backend epic — an API artifact consumed by another epic's code, so serial. Not a configuration reference. |\n37\t| E001 → E003 | Step 5.5 tiered delivery | The Tier-2 schema reconciliation modifies the Tier-1 report computation in the same codebase — it is the dependency-gated continuation of the same workstream, so serial. |\n38\t| E001 → E004, E002 → E004, E003 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | The docs-authoring epic is blocked by every Implementation epic; documentation describes what was built. |\n39\t| (no edge) E002 ∥ E003 | Rule 14/15 | Different repos and surfaces. The stable API shields the Dashboard views from metering-schema drift, so the Dashboard work does not need the schema integration to finish — parallel. |\n40\t\n41\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n42\t\n43\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Status | Notes |\n44\t|-----|----------|---------|--------|-------|\n45\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E001, E002, E003 | Covered | Split by boundary: computation + stable API (E001, Distributed Workloads), administrator view (E002, Dashboard). Real-data delivery completes with the Tier-2 integration (E003). |\n46\t| HLR-2 | P1 | E001 | Covered — **bundled** | CSV export and the stable API are retrieval formats of the same per-project GPU-hour report data and API surface the P0 computation produces. Splitting would make two epics build the same data structure and API endpoint; the P1 label reads as relative value, not a deferral signal. |\n47\t| HLR-3 | P1 | E002 | Covered — **bundled** | The current-month-vs-Kueue-quota view renders the same consumption data in the same administrator view surface as the P0 report view and reuses its project scoping — it is not independently schedulable, so it stays bundled with the P0 view work. |\n48\t| HLR-4 | P2 | E004 | Covered | docs-authoring epic; priority derived from strategy `priority: Major` → P1 per the docs-authoring priority exception (not an inversion — see Health Warnings). |\n49\t\n50\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n51\t\n52\t## Tiered Delivery\n53\t\n54\tThe single external dependency — the metering data and its schema, owned by the in-flight RHAISTRAT-9990 strategy — has uncertain timing. Partial value is deliverable without it, so the strategy splits:\n55\t\n56\t- **Tier 1 (independent, starts now)**: E001 (report backend, stable API, CSV export) and E002 (Dashboard admin views), built and validated against the metering data contract as currently drafted by RHAISTRAT-9990. Risk-reduction value: the reporting stack exists and is tested before the metering data lands.\n57\t- **Tier 2 (dependency-gated)**: E003 — reconciliation with the finalized metering data schema and end-to-end validation on real metering output. Gated on RHAISTRAT-9990 landing; modeled as a precondition with tiered delivery (Rule 21: third party resolves — no separate epic for the dependency itself, no Investigation epic).\n58\t\n59\t## Open Question Handling\n60\t\n61\tThe strategy's single open question — \"the metering data schema finalizes only when RHAISTRAT-9990 lands\" — was evaluated against the Step 3 decision rule. The answer does not change **which** epics exist (backend, views, integration, docs all exist regardless), and the resolution is produced by a third-party strategy, not by any investigation this team can run: no readable artifact holds the final answer yet, and the gating event is external coordination. Per Rule 21 this is modeled as a precondition with tiered delivery (see above) rather than an Investigation epic. The residual risk — schema drift between draft contract and final schema — is absorbed by E003, whose scope exists precisely to reconcile that drift.\n62\t\n63\t## Component Mapping Notes\n64\t\n65\t- The strategy lists affected components (Dashboard, Distributed Workloads) without per-component change or owner detail. Owners were taken from the architecture context files (`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/dashboard.md`: RHOAI Dashboard; `.../distributed-workloads.md`: Distributed Workloads).\n66\t- **Report backend assigned to Distributed Workloads (non-obvious mapping):** the reporting backend (per-project GPU-hour aggregation, stable API, CSV export) is assigned to the Distributed Workloads canonical component because it computes over workload GPU consumption and the Kueue quota model — the Distributed Workloads domain. `dashboard.md` states the Dashboard \"owns no data plane\" and uses \"Kubernetes API only; no private component APIs\", so a time-range aggregation API is not a Dashboard-internal artifact. This epic's work spans the strategy components Dashboard + Distributed Workloads; Distributed Workloads is the single most-relevant canonical name.\n67\t- **HLR-3 quota side:** the Dashboard reads Kueue quota through its existing CR-based integration model (per `dashboard.md`), so no separate Distributed Workloads epic is needed to expose quota — quota-semantics correctness is an acceptance criterion on the Dashboard epic.\n68\t- **Documentation component:** Documentation is a canonical Jira component with an established Red Hat docs content process; this is content authoring, not provisioning of a new code component, so no onboarding chain is generated (Rule 20 targets new platform components).\n69\t\n70\t## Health Warnings\n71\t\n72\t1. **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no Affected Components change/owner detail, no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, and no Non-Functional Requirements. Epic-level ACs were derived from the HLRs; team assignments came from architecture context. Human verification of team routing recommended.\n73\t2. **External timing risk**: E003 completes only when RHAISTRAT-9990 lands and its schema is final. That schedule is outside this strategy's control; Tier 1 (E001, E002) deliberately proceeds in parallel against the draft contract so the strategy is not blocked.\n74\t3. **\"Stable API\" commitment (HLR-2)**: the strategy requires a stable API for finance tooling but defines no versioning process. The API design in E001 should treat stability/versioning as an explicit design concern; flagged as a scope consideration, not a defect.\n75\t4. **Docs priority reads as an inversion but is not one**: HLR-4 is P2 while the docs epic is P1 — this follows the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy priority Major → P1) and does not push priority onto the implementations.\n76\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8f7af457fe3af600","agent_id":"adbb64701485d60d1","session_id":"9bcc61a9-70a5-417c-9026-158475c20fef","uuid":"e8846bb5-7021-4822-b736-1db939547c88","timestamp":"2026-10-23T19:53:01.757Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-99d16a4811493313","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9018 decomposition"}
