{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b52048d178213ea4","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9018-E001\n3\ttitle: GPU-hour chargeback report backend with stable API and CSV export\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9018\n5\tcomponent: Distributed Workloads\n6\tteam: Distributed Workloads\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies: []\n10\tai_signals:\n11\t  change_specificity: 0\n12\t  pattern_precedent: 0\n13\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n14\t  existing_foundation: 0\n15\t  open_questions: 0\n16\t  external_dependency: -1\n17\t  human_process_gates: 0\n18\t  repo_access: 1\n19\t  architecture_claims: 1\n20\timplementation_type: null\n21\tinvestigation_signals: null\n22\tai_implementability: Medium\n23\tai_implementability_score: 1\n24\tjira_key: null\n25\tbranch: null\n26\tgated_by: null\n27\tgate_failure_impact: null\n28\t---\n29\t# GPU-hour chargeback report backend with stable API and CSV export\n30\t\n31\t## Description\n32\t\n33\tDelivers the report computation backend for administrator-facing GPU chargeback reporting: per-project GPU-hour consumption aggregated over selectable time ranges, computed exclusively from the metering data delivered by the GPU usage metering strategy (RHAISTRAT-9990), and exposed through a stable API that also serves CSV export for finance tooling. This is the Tier-1 build of the chargeback capability: it targets the metering data contract as currently drafted by the metering strategy, so the reporting stack can be built and validated while that strategy is still in flight. It does not re-implement metering collection, and reports are expressed in GPU-hours, not currency.\n34\t\n35\t## Scope\n36\t\n37\t- Per-project GPU-hour consumption aggregation over selectable time ranges, reading the metering data delivered by RHAISTRAT-9990. No metering collection logic is implemented here — collection is owned by the metering strategy and is out of scope for this strategy.\n38\t- A stable API exposing the computed per-project reports for arbitrary time ranges, designed as the contract that the administrator Dashboard views and finance tooling build against. API stability/versioning is an explicit design concern.\n39\t- CSV export of the same report data via the API.\n40\t- Unit and integration tests against representative sample metering data that follows the documented metering data contract.\n41\t- A precise written definition of report semantics (what counts as a GPU-hour, time-range boundary handling, per-project attribution rules) as the authoritative input to validation and documentation.\n42\t\n43\t## Acceptance Criteria\n44\t\n45\t- Given representative metering data that follows the documented metering data contract, the report computation returns correct per-project GPU-hour totals for any selected time range, verified by automated tests with known inputs.\n46\t- The API returns per-project GPU-hour reports for requested time ranges, and its contract is covered by automated contract tests.\n47\t- Requesting CSV export for a time range produces CSV content whose rows and values match the API's report response for the same query.\n48\t- The implementation contains no metering collection pipeline code — all GPU-hour usage input comes from the metering data source.\n49\t- The report-semantics definition (GPU-hour counting, time-range boundaries, per-project attribution) is written down in this epic's deliverable documentation.\n50\t\n51\t## HLR Traceability\n52\t\n53\t- HLR-1 (P0): per-project GPU-hour consumption reports over selectable time ranges — computation and stable API (the administrator view over this data is delivered in the Dashboard component).\n54\t- HLR-2 (P1): reports exportable as CSV and retrievable via a stable API for finance tooling — bundled with the P0 computation because CSV export and the API are retrieval formats of the same report data structure and API surface; splitting would build the same surface twice.\n55\t\n56\t## Risks / Notes\n57\t\n58\t- The metering data schema is owned by RHAISTRAT-9990 and is not final until that strategy lands. This epic builds against the documented draft contract; reconciliation with the final schema is carried by the Tier-2 integration work on this same component, which is gated on the metering strategy landing.\n59\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8f7af457fe3af600","agent_id":"adbb64701485d60d1","session_id":"9bcc61a9-70a5-417c-9026-158475c20fef","uuid":"06abbb23-9ce7-4f8e-b08d-3ef373d4428e","timestamp":"2026-10-23T19:53:05.018Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b52048d178213ea4","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9018 decomposition"}
